① Energy being “consumed” does not mean that energy itself disappears;
energy is converted into forms convenient for use.
After use, it is ultimately converted mainly into internal energy, described in the source as thermal energy.

Thermal power stations obtain energy from fuel;
the source lists coal, combustible gas, petroleum, heavy fuel oil, and oil shale.
It names steam turbines, gas turbines, or internal-combustion engines as machines that drive the generator rotor.

**Applications of high-frequency current** For many applications discussed in the source, alternating current with an oscillation frequency of tens of thousands or hundreds of thousands of hertz is convenient.
This section calls such current high-frequency current;
its stated range is not a single classification boundary for every field.

High-frequency currents and fields also have medical applications;
the source describes heating the surface or deeper regions of parts of the body.
This introduces an energy-conversion principle used in medicine, not instructions for performing high-frequency treatment oneself.
